## Principle 3: Interpretive decisions must be made explicitly, documented honestly, and separated from computational outputs

*What principles emerged for making judgment calls on cultural material that resists simple categorization?*

**Daria:**
- Training a model on your own labels means any pattern in your judgment gets learned and applied at scale across every unlabeled item. That is not a flaw — it is how the method works — but you have to acknowledge it rather than present the output as objective.
- Testing a model on data it was trained on produces an accuracy number that is meaningless. Leave-One-Out cross-validation gives you an honest measure of performance on items the model has never seen — on small datasets, the difference between in-sample and held-out accuracy can be the difference between reporting something true and reporting something misleading.
- Keeping a written reasoning note for each label — not just the label itself — forces you to articulate why, and that articulation reveals when your reasoning is consistent and when it is not. It is also the only way anyone can audit your decisions later.
- When a model scores most items near the uncertain midpoint, that is information about your data, not your subject. It means the text is too sparse or too uniform for the method to find signal. That is a methodological finding worth reporting honestly.

**Adin:**
- If anything was overly repetitive, I immediately removed it. In some cases, there were fields where every entry was the same, so I removed them. I didn't fight the data, though. If trying to remove something caused errors, I left it ('Layer' field)

**Christina:**
- My project showed me that automated classification still depends on human choices. I used keyword rules to classify tone, recovery language, numeric mentions, and causal mentions, but those rules came from my own decisions about which words counted as negative, positive, or causal.
= Keeping the classification rules transparent was important because it made the dataset easier to audit. Unlike an LLM-generated classification, the keyword-based method could be checked by another person because the rules were written directly into the Python script.

**Vedha:**
- In my project, I initially experimented with AI-assisted annotation for describing artworks, but found that it tended to flatten interpretation into generic summaries. This reinforced the importance of keeping interpretive work separate from computational pipelines.
- Removing fields like political impact rating and identity categories made me realize that some of the most important analytical decisions are actually about what not to encode.
- Documentation became as important as the dataset itself, especially in explaining why certain variables were removed or reframed.

**Ananya:**
I learned that there should be as much transparency as possible in the documentation of computational outputs to make it easier for the data to be properly interpreted by users, whether it is the general public attempting to learn more about their elected officials or academics attempting to study female legislative behaviors. As I was writing my report for the initial 50-row dataset, I realized that despite my efforts to provide security for my LLM-augmented dataset by creating a layer of citations for the data found, researchers might still feel uncomfortable utilizing it due to potential inconsistencies in coverage and categorization by the LLMs. This led me to leverage discrete scraping and categorizing methods in my final dataset to showcase the legislative efforts of female legislators. I made it a point to share exactly how many female legislators the dataset covers, which General Assemblies it covers, and how the categorization of whether a bill was in healthcare, education/children, or employment was explicitly made through the categorization provided by the sponsorship reports themselves. This showcases that there is strong consistency in the data and that its findings can share insights about these legislators during this time period, but should not be extrapolated beyond this. I believe that this approach makes the dataset much more approachable for researchers and the public alike while maintaining ethical standards.
